## Response-unit principles

- Preserve all original task data and all printed subparts, not a selected
  numerical subset. Cover meaningful mathematical results, not blanks.
- A full ordered correspondence, decomposition table, interval map or coherent
  paired result is the response unit for a deterministic calculation task.
  Native single is natural when the learner selects one complete result.
- For open example tasks, native multiple is natural for selecting every valid
  coherent example group. Each group contains several numbers and a stated
  target; judging the WHOLE group is different from clicking each number.
  Recognizing examples is not evidence of independently producing them.
- Do not force native multiple onto a deterministic result or native single
  onto a task that genuinely asks for all qualifying groups.
- No question count, option count, per-source quota or preferred number of true
  alternatives is assigned here. Any later grouping must follow the mathematical
  relationship and readable final layout, not printed letters or a quota.
- Splitting a table requires a substantive coherent distinction, such as the
  integer/noninteger and sign boundary behavior, while preserving all inputs.
  Mere row count or a separate printed subpart is not sufficient justification.
- Do not duplicate a solved inference in a second item only to increase coverage
  counts. Check cross-question leakage if two units use the same values or if
  interval/family results would give another item's examples away.

## Prospective mathematical families

### Floor evaluations: 653 and 654

Preferred mode: single for a complete ordered input-to-floor correspondence.
Keep all eight inputs of 653 and all four bracket expressions of 654.
The distinction is the same function expressed as prose in 653 and notation
in 654, so a merge is mathematically possible, not justified merely by proximity.
Separate complete tables are also possible when the final prose/notation task
remains clearer that way. No final number of units is fixed.

The meaningful unit must retain a positive integer, a negative integer,
nonintegers on both sides of zero, and negative multi-digit behavior in the
relevant complete family. Do not replace twelve determinations by twelve
isolated arithmetic clicks. A segmented design must document where each
original input is accounted for and must not discard the integer endpoint case.
Choice recognition will not observe the learner writing each floor calculation.

### Fractional-part evaluations: 655 and 656

Preferred mode: single for a complete coherent fractional-part correspondence.
Retain all eight arguments of 655 and all four brace expressions of 656.
The shared rule x-[x] supplies the mathematical merge relationship.

Do not reduce the task to reading unsigned decimal digits. Preserve integers
with zero fractional part, positive/negative paired magnitudes, the near-integer
negative -100.01 case, the exact rational -7/11 case, and the negative
multi-digit brace expression. Ordered whole results are comparable response
units; any segmentation must keep a mathematically meaningful sign/boundary
comparison rather than a click for each subtraction.
Recognition does not observe independent written subtraction from the floor.

### Paired parts and decompositions: 657 and 658

Preferred mode: single for the complete positive/negative pair in 657 and for
a coherent full decomposition result in 658.

657's two x values form one sign-comparison task: the result for a given x is
the ordered pair ([x],{x}), not two unrelated answer slots. Preserve both signs
and require the two coordinates to describe the same source value.

658's eight equalities form a constrained decomposition task: integer first
summand, second summand in [0,1), correct sum. Preserve every source number,
particularly the small negative decimals and the negative mixed number.
This differs from 655's isolated fractional-part task, so merging everything
solely because the same floor rule is used would hide the decomposition demand.
The complete-result unit should test both terms and their relationship, not
one missing decimal per equality.

Recognition cannot certify independently constructing and writing the
decompositions. It can still test their mathematical constraints and complete
results. Intermediate written derivations are not observed by a choice response.

### Open floor examples: 659

Preferred mode: multiple over coherent proposed example-group assignments.
A response group relates one requested integer part to SEVERAL numbers; the
learner judges whether the entire group satisfies that target.
No options or example-group alternatives are constructed in this stage.

All six original targets must survive: 14, 1, 0, -1, -5, -10.
Later visible groups must provide meaningful valid examples for every target,
not just ask learners to reject a target with no valid examples available.
Integer examples and negative nonintegers must remain represented; negative
groups must expose the interval direction rather than truncation toward zero.
Several possible example groups can be valid; do not frame the frozen author's
witness list as the unique answer or as an exhaustive finite set.

Mathematical justification: each target's admissible fiber is [n,n+1), and
checking every member of a group requires that interval and its endpoints.
Group recognition is useful but loses selecting one's own real numbers from
each interval and explaining their validity without offered candidates.
Record that production loss in coverage; never report the original generation
task as independently demonstrated.
If a compact complete example catalogue is later used instead, reconsider the
mode on its actual response unit and preserve the same nonuniqueness and loss.

### Complete floor fibers: 660

Preferred mode: single for the entire five-target map to real solution sets.
Targets 8, 1, 0, -1, -17 and all left/right endpoints must remain visible.

The unit is a map of complete half-open intervals, not five endpoint clicks
and not one sampled value for each interval. The common floor preimage law
mathematically unifies the five subparts. Recognition loses independently
writing the interval notation, but it can assess the entire set and endpoints.
Do not merge it with 659 in a way that turns a full-set problem into witness
selection or supplies 659's solutions through another item's visible result.

### Number-line sets: 661

Preferred mode: single for a completed paired representation of [3,4) and
[-3,-2), with the original two conditions distinguished.
The mathematical unit includes orientation, coordinate labels, filled left
endpoints, open right endpoints and the exact marked segments.

A later image-enabled path could recognize a completed paired number-line
representation. No image exists in this stage, no source image is missing,
and no final renderer layout or asset is guessed.
If the later authorized workflow cannot supply and independently review actual
candidate graphics, an algebraic pair of intervals can retain the set/endpoint
mathematics only. It must then name TWO losses: reading the drawn number-line
representation and independently drawing/marking it. Do not describe text-only
interval recognition as graphical coverage.

Even with reviewed recognition graphics, independent construction remains
unobserved: placing -3 to the left of -2, choosing coordinates and marking the
two open/filled endpoints oneself. The later semantic gate must judge whether
the explicit production loss is acceptable for the intended task.

### Integer shifts: 662

Preferred mode: single for a coherent full result linking both ordered triples
to the universal fractional-part comparison.

Preserve a's arguments x, x+4, x-12 with x=3.7; preserve b's arguments
x, x-50, x+13 with x=-7.4. Retain c's full quantifiers: x in R and n in Z.
The two triples are evidence of the same integer-translation structure, not
six independent cell exercises. The universal result must not be replaced by
a claim only about the sample numbers or positive shifts.

The frozen proof establishes [x+n]=[x]+n and {x+n}={x}; a response unit
must not confuse invariance of the fractional part with invariance of the floor.
Do not print the already-computed triples as hidden-answer guidance in a stem
and then ask only about the general law. Keep source data, not solved data,
as givens. All requested results must be genuinely assessed by the full unit.
Recognition does not observe writing the calculations or independently
constructing the general argument; no source demand to write a formal proof
is being invented.

### Open fractional-part examples: 663

Preferred mode: multiple over coherent example groups linked to the required
fractional part. Judge a group's several members together, not each member
through a separate yes/no click.

All three original targets must survive: 0.9, 0.02 and 0.
The later response must accommodate positive and negative examples for nonzero
targets and positive/zero/negative integers for zero. It must not silently
restrict x to [0,1) or a family parameter to nonnegative integers.
Provide meaningful valid groups for every target; do not treat a finite group
as the full admissible set or require the frozen author's particular witnesses.

The mathematical unit tests x=k+r with integer k and fixed r in [0,1).
This discrete family is a different inverse problem from 659's entire interval
for fixed floor, so proximity or parallel wording does not justify merging the
two tasks. In particular, r=0 requires exactly integers, not an interval.
Recognizing groups loses independently inventing several examples and verifying
their fractional parts without candidate guidance. Record that loss explicitly.

### Exponential graphs and comparisons: 664

Preferred mode: single for a coherent completed two-curve comparison result
that retains BOTH curves and the equation plus BOTH strict inequalities.
A purely algebraic comparison must not be reported as full graph-task coverage.

Mathematical requirements for any later graphical response:
- Both exact bases 0.7 and 0.4, common real domain, positive ranges, the common
  intercept (0,1), decreasing exponential shapes and y=0 approached from above.
- The entire sign distinction: 0.7^x below 0.4^x for x<0, equal at zero,
  above for x>0; no further crossings.
- The equation's solution set and the two strict-inequality solution sets,
  with x=0 excluded from both strict cases and noninteger x included.
- The negative-x side, curve labels and shared coordinate scales. Merely
  comparing heights at x=1 would omit half the source comparison.

These requirements come from the frozen source solution, NOT a supplied source
image, invented missing scan, chosen final viewport or copied final graph.
No candidate image, figure ID, alternative profile or visible stem exists now.
When later authorized and genuinely needed, use ld-s10y-image's deterministic
source.authoredBasis route because the original exercise has no supplied figure.
Keep authored graphics separate from the official seed. Read the current shared
image batch rules and use batches of at most eight actual images at that time;
obtain the required independent actual-image, hash-bound review. Not now.

Concrete recognition losses even if graphics are eventually supplied:
independently choosing coordinate scales, computing/placing reference points,
labelling two curves, drawing their decreasing shapes and asymptotic behavior,
and constructing their crossing/order correctly without a supplied completion.
Recognizing a completed drawing is not evidence of doing those things oneself.

If only text-based comparisons can be authored later, additionally record the
omission of visual graph recognition and reading the solutions from graphs.
The equality and inequalities can still be assessed mathematically, but that
is partial graph-task coverage, not a claim that sketching was preserved.
An essential unaccepted graph-production loss would block this affected item
at semantic review; it would not erase the completed source mathematics.
Do not create separate repetitive questions whose solutions disclose the
relative-order result to one another.

